contentText:
This week in dance music: Steve Aoki and Calvin Harris both made noise in the legal world, with Aoki facing a class-action lawsuit over his partnership with the gaming company MetaZoo and Harris winning $13.5 million in the first bout of a legal battle with his former business manager.

Meanwhile, Robyn revealed the moment she knew Elon Musk was, as she says, &#8220;tacky,&#8221; Splice revealed its top genres of 2025 and ABBA scored its first No. 1 on a Billboard dance chart, with Gold: Greatest Hits  rising to No. 1 on Top  Dance  Albums.

And to close it out, these are the best new dance projects of the week.

Skrillex, Kora

It&#8217;s ambient but make it Skrillex on Kora , the producer&#8217;s new three-track EP. Populated by collaborators like Whitearmor, swedm® and other affiliates who also appeared on Skrill&#8217;s most recent studio album last April, the eight-and-a-half-minute project is experimental, meandering and fairly light — music for the acid trip rather than the rave. It&#8217;s best listened to in headphones good enough to reveal the many subtle nuances of the sound design and to also emphasize the punchier, weightier moments, including Skrillex&#8217;s own singing. Kora is out on OWSLA/Atlantic Recording Corporation.

Slayyyter, &#8220;Dance&#8230;&#8221;

Electropop hero Slayyyter unfurls a dancefloor siren call with &#8220;Dance,&#8221; designed to make you do just that. Pulsing, breathy and the right kind of ominous, the track folds in elements of French touch and finds Slayyyter declaring that &#8220;I kind of hate you but, it doesn&#8217;t matter, let me daaaaance,&#8221; a nod to the eternal shake-it-off power of the club. Don&#8217;t sleep on the stylish disco-meets-crime-scene-meets-house-party-meets- Carrie music video, either.

Crankdat, &#8220;Movement&#8221;

Ohio native Crankdat is steadily become one of the key bass producers of the moment, a position he further solidifies with his latest, &#8220;Movement.&#8221; The heavy, crunchy, headbanging type of dubstep that seems like it might ultimately send you cross-eyed, the viral hit — which features vocals from Linney — is officially out today on the producer&#8217;s own Crank It Up! Records.

Illenium, Bastille & Dabin, &#8220;Feel Alive &#8220;

The Odyssey continues with &#8220;Feel Alive,&#8221; the eighth single from Illenium&#8217;s forthcoming album, out Feb. 6. Made with Bastille and longtime associate Dabin, the song is classic Illenium-core, which is to say it&#8217;s big, anthemic and built around his signature future bass builds and drops. The new song comes in tandem with the announcement of Odyssey &#8216;s whopping 19-song tracklist, which includes collaborations like Kid Cudi, Zeds Dead and Bring Me the Horizon. &#8220;Feel Alive&#8221; is out on Republic Records.

Shimza & Elderbrook, &#8220;Falling&#8221;

South Africa&#8217;s Shimza links with English producer Elderbrook for &#8220;Falling,&#8221; an Afro house cut that maintains the genre&#8217;s scintillating percussion while also growing to anthem size via its big synths and singalong vocals. The song is out on Helix Records.

Flight School, &#8220;Up2Night&#8221;

Canadian duo Flight School drop an electro heater in &#8220;Up2Night,&#8221; a collaboration with Australian vocalist Gigi Russ. Textural and driving, this loosie single more or less demands your presence on the dancefloor, and is out on Palm Tree Records.
